EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - STOPPED SCHOOL BUS - NIGHT

Thick white fog. The bus idles, hazards ticking orange. Four teenagers inside, no driver.

CORMAC, 16, stands in the aisle with a flashlight. TEO, 17, crouches behind a seat with a cracked phone. ISLA, 16, grips the emergency window latch. NKECHI, 15, sits rigid at the front, hands in her lap.

He sweeps the beam down the aisle. Nothing but empty seats. She stares at the dashboard, which glows green. He thumbs his phone and curses. She presses the window glass with her palm. He catches her eye. She shakes her head.

CORMAC
Where did Mr. Delroy go?

TEO
He said he'd check the engine.

ISLA
That was an hour ago.

NKECHI
(not turning)
Stop talking.

TEO
What?

NKECHI
Something is on the roof.

They all look up. A slow drag of fingers across metal, from the back of the bus to the front. He raises the flashlight. She pulls the latch. He stops her wrist. The drag stops above the door.

CORMAC
(whisper)
Nobody breathe.

The door lever, with no one near it, turns by itself. The folding door hisses open. Fog pours in like milk. Footsteps climb one step. Then two. Then a voice, warm and wet and familiar, speaks from the dark between the seats.

NKECHI
(in a flat, wrong voice, Mr. Delroy's)
Everybody have their seatbelts on?

Nobody has. They all scream.
